{
  "dom_id": "step-q99959",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the limit at $x=-1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & \\text{\u00a0for\u00a0}\\,x<-1 \\\\ -2\\, & \\text{\u00a0for\u00a0}\\,x=-1 \\\\ x+2\\, & \\text{\u00a0for\u00a0}\\,x>-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$",
